   "source": [
    "### Handling Metastable Stuck States\n",
    "\n",
    "A challenge in automating temperature sweeps is the physical reality of the deep ordered phase ($T < T_c$). In this regime, a random initial configuration often nucleates multiple domains of opposite spin that become topologically trapped. These \"stuck\" states form long-lived metastable plateaus with $|M| < 1$ and very low variance, preventing the random and ordered traces from ever meeting within a reasonable simulation time.\n",
    "\n",
    "To ensure high throughput and physical correctness, VibeSpin's equilibration protocol includes **Quasi-Steady Stuck Detection**. If the system identifies that the ordered-start trace has settled into a stable ground state ($|M| \\approx 1$) while the random-start trace is stranded in a metastable plateau, it declares the point equilibrated and automatically falls back to the stable ordered-start simulation for all measurements. This ensures the thermodynamic curves reflect the true equilibrium phase rather than initialization artifacts. Furthermore, the sweep is parallelized at the level of individual $(T, \\text{seed})$ points, ensuring that slow points near $T_c$ do not block the overall progress of the simulation."
   ]
